
export anchor Story_7fbf4d21d67cfd7e:
    userPrompt:
        \\\
        Muse Code is a fourth harness a project can run with, alongside
        Claude Code, Codex, and OpenCode. The spec has nothing on Muse Code
        yet, so this is new. Before changing anything, find out from Muse
        Code's own documentation or its --help how it is run one-off and
        non-interactively: its command, how it takes a prompt on standard
        input, how it streams what it does as JSON, how it carries on a
        conversation, whether it takes a system prompt and a model, and the
        folder it reads agentic Markdown from. If any of these can't be
        found, stop and say what is missing.

        Once this is done, a project whose harness is Muse Code sends every
        run to it: tasks, questions, the palette's file search, and the
        notes, commit messages, and prompt titles. Each run happens as
        spec/scope/harness-integration/index.pi#HarnessIntegration.harnesses
        describes for the other three. Muse Code's streamed events are read
        into the same reply, tool calls, usage, and result. The project's
        system prompt is given the way Muse Code takes one, or ahead of the
        prompt and marked off from it if it takes none, as Codex and
        OpenCode do. Each lane's conversation is carried on with Muse Code's
        own resume. HARNESS_DIRECTORY stands for Muse Code's folder, as
        spec/scope/prompt-mode/mode-system-prompts/index.pi#ModeSystemPrompts
        says for the others. Its command is found on the user's own PATH, as
        spec/scope/application/index.pi#Application says. A run that fails
        or can't start says why in Muse Code's own words, as
        spec/scope/harness-integration/index.pi#HarnessIntegration.failures
        requires. A Muse Code run is sandboxed like any other run. Where
        Muse Code can't do something (be fed more while it works, fork a
        conversation, take a small model), the spec says so, as it does for
        Codex and OpenCode.
        \\\

    mode: combined

    model: opus

    addedSteps:
        step1:
            \\\
            mode: code
            model: opus

            Muse Code can be picked as a project's harness. The Agent section of
            spec/scope/settings-window/index.pi#SettingsWindow.agent has a fourth
            row, "Muse Code", with its command muted beside it and "Not
            installed" when the command can't be found. Picking it saves it with
            the project, as spec/scope/project-data/index.pi#ProjectData says.
            The Harness group in
            spec/scope/new-project/index.pi#NewProject.agents lists only agents
            Belay has an adapter for. The spec has nothing on a Muse Code
            adapter. If @piton/belay has one, add Muse Code as an agent there,
            along with its adapter in the written piton.config.pi. If it doesn't,
            leave New Project as it is and say so. Muse Code is then picked
            afterwards in Settings. A project with no saved harness still falls
            back by its adapters, as
            spec/scope/harness-integration/index.pi#HarnessIntegration.harnesses
            says.
            \\\
        step2:
            \\\
            mode: code
            model: opus

            Muse Code conversations last across restarts, machine moves, and
            cleanups, just as the other harnesses' do in
            spec/scope/harness-integration/index.pi#HarnessIntegration.context.
            After every Muse Code run, its session is copied into the project's
            data next to the run's history record. Before a conversation is
            carried on, the session is put back where Muse Code looks for it,
            under the same id. If there is no copy, the request is sent again as
            a new conversation. The sandbox in spec/scope/sandbox/index.pi#Sandbox
            lets a Muse Code run reach its own session folder and login on the
            host, and nothing more. The spec doesn't say where Muse Code keeps
            its sessions yet. Find out and write it in.
            \\\
